// oleg.ae/for-laura: explains to Laura Moiseja (Boldane client) why her 2026-10-14 interviews produced 29 posts.
// Unlisted, noindex. Generated by a one-off script in a Boldane admin session (2026-10-20).
export const HTML = "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\"><meta name=\"robots\" content=\"noindex, nofollow\"><title>What happened on 14 October</title><meta name=\"description\" content=\"Why your interviews on 14 October turned into 29 posts, and what to do next time.\">\n<style>\n:root{--bg:#f6f7f9;--card:#fff;--ink:#14181f;--mut:#5d6573;--line:#e3e6eb;--blue:#2863f0;--blue-bg:#eaf0fe;--amber:#b45309;--amber-bg:#fdf3e4;--green:#15803d;--green-bg:#e8f6ed}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){:root{--bg:#0b0f16;--card:#121823;--ink:#e6e9ee;--mut:#9aa3b2;--line:#242c3a;--blue:#6b93ff;--blue-bg:#16213a;--amber:#f0a654;--amber-bg:#2a1f12;--green:#5bd28a;--green-bg:#122619}}\n*{box-sizing:border-box}html{-webkit-text-size-adjust:100%}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);font:16px/1.55 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,\"Segoe UI\",Inter,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif}\nmain{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 16px 64px}\nh1{font-size:28px;line-height:1.2;margin:0 0 12px;letter-spacing:-.01em}\nh2{font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;margin:40px 0 8px}\np{margin:0 0 12px}.lead{font-size:18px;color:var(--ink)}.mut{color:var(--mut)}\n.card{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:18px}\n.tl{list-style:none;margin:16px 0 0;padding:0;border-left:2px solid var(--line)}\n.tl li{position:relative;padding:0 0 16px 18px}.tl li:before{content:\"\";position:absolute;left:-7px;top:6px;width:12px;height:12px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--card);border:2px solid var(--mut)}\n.tl li.b:before{border-color:var(--blue);background:var(--blue)}.tl li.a:before{border-color:var(--amber);background:var(--amber)}\n.tl time{font-weight:600;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;margin-right:6px}\n.cmp{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px;margin:16px 0}\n.stat{border-radius:14px;padding:16px}.stat b{display:block;font-size:34px;line-height:1.1}.stat span{font-size:14px}\n.s-new{background:var(--green-bg);color:var(--green)}.s-old{background:var(--amber-bg);color:var(--amber)}\n.post{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;margin:0 0 10px}\n.post summary{list-style:none;cursor:pointer;padding:14px 16px;display:block}.post summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}\n.topic{display:block;font-weight:650;margin:4px 0 2px}.hook{display:block;color:var(--mut);font-size:15px}\n.more{display:inline-block;margin-top:8px;font-size:14px;color:var(--blue);font-weight:600}.post[open] .more{display:none}\n.full{padding:0 16px 14px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);margin-top:4px;padding-top:12px;font-size:15px}.full p{margin:0 0 10px}\n.tag{display:inline-block;font-size:12px;font-weight:600;color:var(--mut);background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;padding:1px 8px}\n.grp{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;margin:0 0 10px}\n.grp.hot{border-color:var(--amber)}.grph{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:8px}.grph .topic{margin:0;flex:1 1 auto}\n.count{font-weight:700;font-size:14px;color:var(--amber);white-space:nowrap}\n.grp ul{margin:10px 0 0;padding-left:18px;color:var(--mut);font-size:15px}.grp li{margin:0 0 6px}\n.tip{background:var(--blue-bg);border-radius:14px;padding:18px}.tip ol{margin:8px 0 0;padding-left:20px}.tip li{margin:0 0 8px}\n.sig{margin-top:32px;color:var(--mut)}\n@media (max-width:420px){h1{font-size:24px}.stat b{font-size:28px}}\n</style></head><body><main>\n<p class=\"mut\">For Laura \u00b7 from Oleg</p>\n<h1>What happened with your posts on 14 October</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">You did two interviews that day and ended up with 29 posts, a lot of them about the same thing. Here is exactly why, and it is my fault, not yours.</p>\n<div class=\"card\"><p><b>In the summer we rebuilt how posts are made from an interview.</b> The new system reads the whole conversation, finds each separate topic you talked about, writes one post per topic, and checks every post against what you actually said. That makes much better posts, but it takes longer: about 45 to 60 minutes instead of 5 to 15.</p><p style=\"margin:0\">I never told you about this change. So when nothing showed up after half an hour, it looked broken, and you did the natural thing and pressed \"Generate Content\". That button starts the <b>old</b> system, which makes 10 to 15 posts per conversation without those checks. I should have told you in advance. Sorry about that.</p></div>\n\n<h2>Your day, step by step</h2>\n<p class=\"mut\">Norway time</p>\n<ul class=\"tl\">\n<li class=\"b\"><time>12:34</time>Interview 1 ends (10 minutes, the intern and the IQ test).</li>\n<li class=\"b\"><time>12:43</time>The new system gives you 1 post about it.</li>\n<li class=\"b\"><time>12:56</time>Interview 2 ends (21 minutes: your daughter's school, juniors and AI, adaptable candidates, Latvia vs Norway).</li>\n<li class=\"a\"><time>13:29</time>After 33 minutes with nothing new, you press \"Generate Content\" on both interviews. The old system starts.</li>\n<li class=\"a\"><time>13:34</time>Old-system posts start arriving. 24 of them by 14:07.</li>\n<li class=\"b\"><time>13:54</time>The new system finishes interview 2: 4 posts, one per topic.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Side by side</h2>\n<div class=\"cmp\"><div class=\"stat s-new\"><b>5 posts</b><span>New system \u00b7 5 different topics</span></div><div class=\"stat s-old\"><b>24 posts</b><span>Old button \u00b7 14 of them retell the intern story</span></div></div>\n\n<h2>What the new system made</h2>\n<p class=\"mut\">One post per topic you talked about. Tap any one to read it.</p>\n<details class=\"post\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Call 1</span><span class=\"topic\">The intern and the IQ test</span><span class=\"hook\">I had an intern score the lowest possible result on an IQ test. Her reaction told me more than the score did.</span><span class=\"more\">Read the post</span></summary><div class=\"full\"><p>I had an intern score the lowest possible result on an IQ test. Her reaction told me more than the score did.</p><p>Most people don&#x27;t want to talk about this.</p><p>I use a lot of tests in recruitment. Sales tests, personality tests, tests to read the environment in a company.</p><p>But I really like IQ tests, because they show the overall growth potential of a person.</p><p>We don&#x27;t even know what positions will be needed in 5 or 10 years. High IQ gives you the ability to learn fast, adapt fast, solve problems. That&#x27;s the most important thing right now.</p><p>And if we&#x27;re talking about AI, AI itself causes a lot of problems in companies, not only solutions. Before the solutions, there are problems. You need sharp minds to solve those.</p><p>People with lower IQs struggle more at the workplace. They feel pushed down, tense, overwhelmed by situations that are completely normal in a professional environment.</p><p>At first she was shocked, she knew she was trying her best. Then she said she didn&#x27;t believe in the test. Which is protection, and honestly, that reaction just confirmed the result was true.</p><p>A few days later, a plan we had changed last minute, like it does in every company. She couldn&#x27;t deal with it.</p><p>Couldn&#x27;t focus on anything else. That wasn&#x27;t lack of experience, I was right there supporting her.</p><p>It was a limited ability to adapt, to stay structured, to trust herself.</p><p>Meanwhile another employee, slightly higher IQ, just stepped in and solved the whole thing. Wasn&#x27;t even her job.</p><p>I&#x27;ve had interns who were smarter and more capable than actual professionals, because of this exact thing. Higher IQ, higher interest, higher motivation.</p><p>So yes, it&#x27;s okay to have a lower IQ. But we need to talk about this more.</p><p>We need to sharpen our minds, learn more, upgrade how we grow, because that&#x27;s the bridge to becoming someone who can actually handle what&#x27;s coming.</p><p>Founders, are you testing for this when you hire? And if you know you&#x27;re not the sharpest in the room, are you doing anything to fix that?</p></div></details><details class=\"post\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Your daughter&#x27;s school and the jobs nobody can predict</span><span class=\"hook\">My daughter&#x27;s school has data proving no one can predict what jobs exist in 15 years, but you&#x27;re still hiring for today&#x27;s titles.</span><span class=\"more\">Read the post</span></summary><div class=\"full\"><p>My daughter&#x27;s school has data proving no one can predict what jobs exist in 15 years, but you&#x27;re still hiring for today&#x27;s titles.</p><p>I visited my daughter&#x27;s school recently. She&#x27;s in first grade.</p><p>The employee walking me through it didn&#x27;t mention math levels or exam scores once.</p><p>What she said instead: they have data, based on huge market research, that we can&#x27;t even imagine what kind of positions are gonna be open in the future.</p><p>So the first years are built around how to adapt in new situations, how to be brave, how to talk properly, how to communicate, how to collaborate in different environments with different people.</p><p>Nobody can name the job titles coming. Not the school. Not us. Not the companies hiring right now.</p><p>I&#x27;m also seeing it in the kids. They compare themselves on soft skills now, not grades.</p><p>10-15 years ago none of us thought AI would answer a simple question, or that we&#x27;d have agents replacing assistants.</p><p>Math still matters. Hard subjects still sharpen the brain. But the school building the next generation stopped measuring kids by exam scores a while ago.</p><p>Makes me wonder what you&#x27;re still measuring candidates by.</p></div></details><details class=\"post\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Juniors with AI are not a cheap shortcut</span><span class=\"hook\">Hire a junior, hand them AI, pay them less.</span><span class=\"more\">Read the post</span></summary><div class=\"full\"><p>Hire a junior, hand them AI, pay them less.</p><p>Then wonder why the output doesn&#x27;t double.</p><p>I hear this logic a lot in hiring calls. Give them the AI tools, pay junior rate, they won&#x27;t even need to work full-time.</p><p>No one&#x27;s goal is to work less. The goal is always to generate more, no matter what.</p><p>And the data backs up what&#x27;s already happening.</p><p>Employment for 22 to 25 year olds in software engineering, marketing, customer service is now sitting about 19% below where it would be if it kept pace with every other age group.</p><p>Not fired. Just never hired.</p><p>I think this just highlights where companies were already going.</p><p>Less focused on someone with 20, 25 years of experience. More focused on late 20s to mid-30s, the strong mid to senior specialist.</p><p>Less patience for onboarding a junior, mostly in software development, not every position.</p><p>My clients tell me this too. No, I don&#x27;t want that young age person, AI can replace them anyway.</p><p>I push back on that.</p><p>Young people are often more flexible, more willing to try new things.</p><p>Not always. But often enough that the blanket no doesn&#x27;t hold up.</p></div></details><details class=\"post\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">How candidates adapt mid-answer</span><span class=\"hook\">I ask candidates if cold calls still work in sales.</span><span class=\"more\">Read the post</span></summary><div class=\"full\"><p>I ask candidates if cold calls still work in sales.</p><p>The ones who adapt mid-answer get the job.</p><p>A good answer sounds like this: yes, based on this industry, cold calls work.</p><p>But if someone isn&#x27;t answering, I&#x27;d try cold email, social outreach, events, or work closely with marketing to reach them another way.</p><p>One answer means they think in one layer.</p><p>Two or three means they adapt to whatever the role throws at them.</p><p>Hard skills don&#x27;t hold up that long anyway. You learn them, then you learn them again. Maybe ten times in one lifetime.</p><p>A law degree doesn&#x27;t mean you know the law forever. The EU is still writing AI law years after it took effect, so lawyers now have to go back and dig deeper than just what they studied.</p><p>I&#x27;ve hired across clients in different countries.</p></div></details><details class=\"post\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Latvia vs Norway: teams that never stop</span><span class=\"hook\">Teams that never stop doing lose people faster.</span><span class=\"more\">Read the post</span></summary><div class=\"full\"><p>Teams that never stop doing lose people faster.</p><p>No time to think, no joy left in the job.</p><p>I moved from Latvia to Norway.</p><p>Latvians work around 1,601 hours a year. Norwegians, about 1,427.</p><p>Norway still moves forward, still hits its goals, still works with high capacity and quality.</p><p>What struck me: they make time to brainstorm.</p><p>In Latvia, and in every other country I&#x27;ve worked in, people don&#x27;t have that time.</p><p>Everyone feels they need to be doing something, all the time. So the most important phase gets skipped: preparation, talking, brainstorming before any action.</p><p>I had a client conversation that was supposed to be interview style.</p><p>No classic questions. We just started brainstorming together, and we immediately felt that we were on the same page.</p><p>That kind of conversation makes decisions faster, not slower. By the time a decision needs to be made, you already have the information.</p><p>The always-doing culture has a cost: higher turnover. People don&#x27;t get time to recharge, to generate new ideas, to find the joy in the job.</p><p>It&#x27;s a cultural thing. Everyone focused on the road, not the destination.</p><p>Companies don&#x27;t even notice that&#x27;s what they&#x27;re doing. They just want to control everything.</p><p>I understand that urge. But that&#x27;s the difference.</p></div></details>\n\n<h2>What the old button added</h2>\n<p class=\"mut\">Grouped by topic. The old system has no idea what the new one already wrote, and it has to come up with 10 to 15 posts even from a 10-minute call. So it squeezes one story into many posts and fills the gaps with details you never said.</p>\n<div class=\"grp hot\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 1</span><span class=\"topic\">The intern and the IQ test</span><span class=\"count\">14 posts</span></div><ul><li>Are you testing for cognitive ability when you hire? Or just hoping for the best?</li><li>An intern of mine scored the lowest possible result on an IQ test.</li><li>A candidate scored the lowest possible result on the IQ test we use for hiring.</li><li>Founders, do you test how fast someone actually thinks before you hire them?</li><li>Do you believe high IQ is actually valid in the workplace?</li><li>Most recruiters test sales skills, personality, team fit.</li><li>I had an intern with the lowest possible score on an IQ test. Here&#x27;s what that actually looked like day to day.</li><li>Saying IQ matters in hiring feels almost illegal right now.</li><li>An intern scored the lowest possible result on an IQ test I gave her.</li><li>In recruitment, I&#x27;ve noticed a pattern with lower IQ candidates: they struggle more at work, even when nothing went wrong.</li><li>The intern froze. The employee, whose job had nothing to do with it, jumped in and fixed it in days.</li><li>An intern took an IQ test. Lowest possible score.</li><li>AI itself causes many, many problems in companies.</li><li>Nobody knows what jobs will even exist in 5 or 10 years.</li></ul></div><div class=\"grp hot\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Latvia vs Norway</span><span class=\"count\">4 posts</span></div><ul><li>Slow is winning in Norway. Fewer hours, same deadlines met. Change my mind.</li><li>A team I know in Norway works way fewer hours than we do in Latvia.</li><li>Companies obsessed with control are focused on the road, not the destination.</li><li>High turnover isn&#x27;t a people problem. It&#x27;s nobody giving the team room to breathe.</li></ul></div><div class=\"grp\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Your daughter&#x27;s school</span><span class=\"count\">3 posts</span></div><ul><li>A teacher at my daughter&#x27;s first grade pulled me aside: kids today aren&#x27;t comparing grades anymore, they&#x27;re comparing soft skills.</li><li>A school told me they can&#x27;t name the jobs they&#x27;re preparing my daughter for.</li><li>My daughter&#x27;s first grade teacher never once mentioned math scores. She talked about adaptability, bravery, and collaboration instead.</li></ul></div><div class=\"grp\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Stanford and junior hiring</span><span class=\"count\">1 post</span></div><ul><li>Stanford just found companies aren&#x27;t firing young workers, they just quietly stopped hiring them.</li></ul></div><div class=\"grp\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">An interview with no questions</span><span class=\"count\">1 post</span></div><ul><li>Had a client interview with no interview questions.</li></ul></div><div class=\"grp\"><div class=\"grph\"><span class=\"tag\">Call 2</span><span class=\"topic\">Lawyers and AI</span><span class=\"count\">1 post</span></div><ul><li>You can&#x27;t coast on a law degree anymore.</li></ul></div>\n\n<h2>Next time</h2>\n<div class=\"tip\"><ol>\n<li>Finish the interview and close the page. Your posts will appear on their own in about an hour.</li>\n<li>Please don't press \"Generate Content\". It only adds the old-style posts on top.</li>\n<li>If after 2 hours you still see nothing, message me and I'll check it straight away.</li>\n</ol></div>\n<p class=\"sig\">Thanks for telling me honestly how it felt. That's what helped me find this.<br>Oleg</p>\n</main></body></html>";
